    "body": {
      "type": "object",
      "description": "LinkedIn Lead Sync 'Find Lead Form Responses by Owner', Marketing API 202609. Sources, as served on 2026-10-07: the Lead Sync reference at moniker li-lms-2026-09 (ms.date 2026-07-14, updated_at 2026-07-29); the Lead Sync schema at li-lms-2026-09 (updated_at 2026-06-24); the Lead Sync API migration guide at li-lms-2026-09 (ms.date 2025-06-17, updated_at 2026-04-16; served as li-lms-2026-08 during planning). REQUEST: q=owner, leadType=(leadType:SPONSORED) and limitedToTestLeads=false are literal; limitedToTestLeads=false returns test and real leads together. {acct} is urn%3Ali%3AsponsoredAccount%3A{id}; submittedAtTimeRange takes epoch milliseconds. The reference lists count and start as optional; they are required here because the client always sends them (100 per page; the recovery probe sends count=1). PROJECTION: request.fields is the migration guide's 'Response Decoration Migration' projection, verbatim, and equals connectors/linkedin/client.py LEAD_FIELDS and lead_form_responses_batch.json request.fields. LinkedIn's Postman collection omits owner from it; this contract keeps owner, and live 202609 returns owner under it. The fields query value, percent-decoded once, must equal request.fields. Its commas, colon and parentheses are sent raw: RFC 3986 allows them in a query, so the raw value is already its single URL encoding, and live 202609 rejects the fully percent-encoded value with HTTP 400 'Projected field ... not present in schema' (observed 2026-10-07). The projection decorates each element with form, built from that element's own versionedLeadGenFormUrn. PAGING: page with start += count. paging.total counts the elements on this page, not the whole result: the documented sample shows count 10, total 1 and no links, and live 202609 returned total 10 with a rel=next link at count=10, and 100 elements, total 100 and a next link at count=100. Stop only on a page with fewer than count elements and no next link; start + count >= total alone stops after page 1. The next href reorders fields and submittedAtTimeRange, so build every page from this template instead of following the href. The lead job requests responses submitted since its last cursor minus a 1 h overlap and de-duplicates on element id (AAP 0.3.2). KEY RISK for connectors/linkedin/parser.parse_lead: under request.fields, associatedEntity, leadMetadata and submitter are not projected, and live projected elements omit them. The creative URN comes from associatedEntityInfo.associatedCreative.id and the campaign URN from leadMetadataInfo.sponsoredLeadMetadataInfo.campaign.id, both URN strings, as the migration guide maps them. parse_lead must read the *Info paths first and fall back to associatedEntity.associatedCreative and leadMetadata.sponsoredLeadMetadata.campaign, so it parses both the unprojected documented sample and projected live data. A null or absent creative means 'unknown creative' (AAP 0.5.4.7). EMAIL: the email is the answerDetails.textQuestionAnswer.answer whose questionId is a decorated question with predefinedField EMAIL. It is trimmed, lowercased and hashed with SHA-256; the plaintext is discarded and never stored. The live forms observed ask only WORK_EMAIL, so the parser must also treat WORK_EMAIL as an email question, after EMAIL. CONSISTENCY CHECK: the version comes only from versionedLeadGenFormUrn, because the decoration has no version field. Every questionId in formResponse.answers must appear among form.content.questions; otherwise the lead is lead_match no_email with reason 'form map mismatch' and is never decoded with another version's map. Without a decoration or a stored map for that version the reason is 'unknown form version'. TEST LEADS: elements with testLead=true are dropped at ingest and never stored. PERSONAL DATA: captures under captured/ redact answers, names and member URNs.",
